WEBVTT

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:01.960
For more information visit www.fema.org

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.680
You better take this along, Don. It's going to be cold on the farm.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:33.080
Okay.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.880
How many shirts are you taking?

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:35.340
Three.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.700
I'm taking five.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:37.480
Five?

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.440
Yeah, I told them at the office I might not be back until Tuesday.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.140
We'll get there this afternoon.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.360
That'll give us all Friday, Saturday, Sunday, Monday.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:44.960
We'll make it a...

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:45.980
It's a long, wonderful weekend.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:48.210
Sounds long, all right.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.580
It'll be good for you, Don, after what you've been through.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:54.670
Trees and grass and sweet cider and buttermilk...

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.490
and water from that well that's colder than any other...

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.230
Wait, please. Why this emphasis on liquids?

00:02:58.490 --> 00:02:59.970
Very dull liquids.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:02.400
Sorry, Don.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:08.990
You know, I think it'd be a good idea if you took along my typewriter.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:09.820
What for?

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.420
Right. I'm gonna write there. Get started on that novel.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:15.820
Do you really feel up to riding?

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:16.400
Why not?

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:18.990
I mean, after what you've been through.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.810
I haven't touched the stuff for ten days now.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.370
I know, I know you haven't, Don.

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:25.900
Where is the typewriter?

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.320
In the living room, in the closet.

00:03:34.124 --> 00:03:35.044
Turn it towards the back.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.900
You sure it's in the closet? I can't find it.

00:03:49.484 --> 00:03:50.244
Well, look for the desk.

00:03:55.852 --> 00:03:56.852
Isn't that under your bed?

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:01.360
Did you find it?

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.100
Oh, sure. Sure, here it is.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:06.590
And here's some paper.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:07.550
Tell you what we'll do.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:09.110
We'll fix up a table on the South porch

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:10.430
and nobody will disturb you.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:11.030
I'll see to it.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.300
Only maybe Saturday we can run down to Country Club.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:14.700
I'm not going near that Country Club.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:15.500
Why not?

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:16.800
There's all a bunch of hypocrites.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:17.820
I don't like to be whispered about.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:19.460
Look who's here from New York.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:20.380
The Burnham brothers.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:22.780
Or rather, the nurse and the invalid.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:24.250
Oh, starboard, Don.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:25.390
Nobody there knows about you.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:26.550
No, the minute we get off the train,

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:27.470
the alarm has sounded.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:29.270
The leper is back.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:29.970
How'd you lick a...

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.460
That's Helen. I'll take it.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.270
Where's Helen? I'm glad I made it. I was afraid you'd be gone.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:42.050
Presents.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:44.790
The new Thurber book with comical jokes and pictures.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.210
Nice, quiet little double murder by Agatha Christie.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.230
Cigarettes, chewing gum, and darling.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:50.690
Have a wonderful time.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:53.030
And don't forget, lots of sleep, lots of milk.

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.990
Lots of soda and lots of nice cold water from the well. I know.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:57.150
Bend down.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:05.480
Now I must be going. I'm ten minutes late for the concert already.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:06.760
What concert?

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:10.010
Carnegie Hall. Barbaroli's conducting. They gave me two tickets.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:13.270
Who are you going with? Nobody.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.350
What are they playing?

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:20.810
Brahms' Second Symphony, something by Beethoven, something by Handel, and not one note of Greek.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:21.750
Sounds wonderful.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.090
Goodbye, boys. See you Monday.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:24.110
Tuesday? Wait a minute.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.260
Wick, I just had a crazy idea.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:27.000
As for instance?

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.120
Who says we have to take the 315 train?

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:35.020
on the 6.30. What are you talking about? Well, I just thought if we took a later train, Helen wouldn't

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:40.940
have to go to the concert by herself. She's got two tickets, hasn't she? No, no, I'm not going to upset

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:47.620
any of your plans. You're going on the 3.15. Oh, now, Helen, it's so silly. A whale of a concert and an empty

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:51.460
seat next to you. No, Don, everything's all set. Now, they'll be at the station to meet us and dinner

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:55.200
will be waiting. Put in a call. We'll be taking a later train and dinner at nine o'clock. We'll be in bed

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.860
by 10. Nothing doing. We're going. Wick is right. And don't worry about that empty seat. I'll find myself

00:05:59.860 --> 00:05:59.960
Thank you very much.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.580
A nice, handsome South American millionaire.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:02.380
There you are. Do you hear?

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:05.040
Besides, you have to break our necks anyway to catch the train.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:06.140
Five to three.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.620
You see? Don't be so stubborn, Wick.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:11.270
All right. Go ahead.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:13.820
Just a minute. I'm not going.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:16.510
Then what are we talking about?

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:17.760
Well, I want you to go.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:18.740
You and Helen.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:19.520
Me and Helen.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:21.420
That's the idea. Who likes Brahms? You or I?

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:22.960
Since when don't you like Brahms?

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:24.780
I'll just stick around here and finish packing.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:26.190
Take a little nap, maybe.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:29.060
Nonsense. If anybody goes, Helen's your girl.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:29.980
There's something in that.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.420
And what's more, I don't think you should be left alone.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:35.180
I shouldn't? No. I want to be trusted, is that it?

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:36.900
Really, Don? After what you've been through...

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.380
After what I've been through, I couldn't go to the council.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:41.040
I couldn't face the crowds. I couldn't sit through it with all those people.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.160
Besides, I want to be alone for a couple of hours.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:45.840
I kind of assemble myself. Is that such an extraordinary thing to want?

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.080
Don't act so outraged. Would you mind?

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:49.370
All right. Anything else?

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:57.170
Please, boys. You'll stay right here.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.380
Where would I go? Then you'll be here when we come back.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:06:59.980
I told you I'm not...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.020
leaving this apartment. You told us a good many things, Don. All right, if you don't believe me,

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:07.780
why don't you take my keys? Lock me in like a dog. We've got to trust Don. That's the only way.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:16.350
Sorry, Don. Let's go, Helen. So long, Don. So long. Don.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:36.700
What's this?

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:38.520
It's a whiskey, isn't it?

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:39.200
How did it get there?

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:39.950
I don't know.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:43.390
I suppose it dropped from some cloud or someone was bouncing it against this wall and it got

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:43.890
stuck there.

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:45.610
I guess I must have put it there.

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:46.370
Yes, you must.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:47.700
I don't remember when.

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:50.500
Probably during my last spell, maybe the one before.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:51.000
I don't know.

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:54.310
Don't look at me like that.

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:55.350
It doesn't mean a thing.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:56.840
I didn't know it was there.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.160
Even if I had, I wouldn't have touched it.

00:07:58.550 --> 00:07:59.510
Then you won't mind.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:14.880
You trot along with Helen.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:16.980
Because of that?

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:19.630
You think I want you out of the apartment because of the bottle?

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:21.500
I resent that like the devil.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:24.440
If there's one more word of discussion, I don't leave on your blasted weekend.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:26.090
Let's go, Wick.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:34.830
You'll be good, won't you, Don, darling?

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:37.150
Yes, Helen, only stop watching me all the time, you two.

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:39.750
Let me work it out my way. I'm trying, I'm trying.

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:43.090
No, you're trying, Don. We're both trying.

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:47.170
You're trying not to drink, and I'm trying not to love you.

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:54.580
Come, Don, and tell him we're taking the 6.30.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:55.400
Sure.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.440
Come on, Ellen. He'll be all right.

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:06.440
What if he goes out and buys another bottle?

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:07.880
With what? He hasn't a nickel.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.620
There isn't a store or bar that'll give him five cents worth of credit.

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:12.620
Are you sure he hasn't got another bottle hidden someplace?

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:13.980
Not anymore. He hasn't.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:15.940
I went over to the apartment with a fine-tooth comb.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:17.340
The places he can figure out.

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:29.010
clean up.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:32.110
Well, not today. Does it have to be today?

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:35.650
I ought to change the sheets. And it's my day to vacuum.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:36.660
Come on, Monday.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:39.670
All right, Mr. Bonham. Is your brother in?

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:40.620
No, he isn't.

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:43.810
What about my money? Didn't he leave my money?

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:46.570
What money?

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:49.260
My ten dollars. Didn't he leave it?

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:51.120
Probably. And where would he leave it?

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:52.150
In the kitchen.

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:54.140
Where in the kitchen?

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:55.800
In the sugar bowl.

00:10:58.190 --> 00:10:58.690
Just a minute.

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:16.350
I'm sorry, Mrs. Foley, it isn't there. You must have forgotten.

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:19.310
Oh, put! I wanted to do some shopping.

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:20.750
You'll get it Monday.

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:22.730
All right, Mr. Burnham.

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:48.410
Two bottles of rye.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:49.690
Sorry, Mr. Burnham.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:51.040
What are you sorry about?

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.070
Your brother was in.

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:53.440
That he's not gonna pay for you anymore.

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:54.520
That was the last time.

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:57.690
Two bottles of rye.

00:11:58.760 --> 00:11:59.020
Brent.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:04.660
Mr. Brophy, the cheapest. None of that 12-year-old aged in the wood shishi, not for me.

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:06.440
Liquors all won anyway.

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:08.200
You want a bag? Yes, I want a bag.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.480
Mother said not to sell you anything, even if you did have the money to pay for it.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:17.520
Well, I can't stop anybody, can I? Not unless you're a miner.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.060
I'm not a miner, Mr. Brophy.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.100
Just to ease your conscience, I'm buying this to refill my cigarette lighter.

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:39.700
Yes, sir. Thank you.

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:47.250
Afternoon, Mrs. Debris. Hello, Mr. Burnham.

00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:59.980
My very good friend, Nat, today. Yes, Mr. Boynham.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:04.510
This being an especially fine afternoon, I have decided to ask for your hand in marriage.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:05.510
Look, Mr. Boynton...

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:08.950
If that has to be your attitude, Nat, I shall have to drown my sorrows in a jigger of rye.

00:13:09.070 --> 00:13:09.770
Just one, that's all.

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:11.280
Can't be done, Mr. Boynton.

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:13.840
Can't? Now, let me guess why.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:16.780
My brother was here, undermining my financial structure.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.300
I didn't tell him nothing about the wristwatch you left with your cufflinks.

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:20.380
Thank you very much, Nat.

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:23.580
Today, you'll be glad to know we can barter on a cash basis.

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:25.490
One straight rye.

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:26.310
That was the idea.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:08.400
Don't wipe it away, Nat. Let me have my little vicious circle.

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:12.700
You know, the circle is the perfect geometric figure.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:14.990
No end, no beginning.

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:16.540
What time is it?

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:18.340
Quarter of four.

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:20.370
Good, we have the whole afternoon together.

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:22.490
Let me know when it's a quarter of six.

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:23.480
It's very important.

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:26.890
I'm going to the country for a weekend with my brother.

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:28.500
Hello, Mr. Burnham.

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:29.980
Happy to have you back with me.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:36.550
Hello, Gloria. I wish I could take you along, Nat, you and all that goes with you.

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:39.950
Not that I'm cutting myself off from civilization altogether.

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:48.280
Of course, there arises the problem of transportation into the country.

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:51.480
How to smuggle these two time bombers past the Royal Guard?

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:54.450
I'll roll one bottle and a copy of the Saturday Evening Post

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:56.290
so my brother can discover it like that.

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:58.850
And I want him to discover it because it'll set his mind to these.

00:14:59.370 --> 00:14:59.910
The other bottle?

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:05.370
Come here. That one I'm tucking into my brother's suitcase.

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:08.620
He shall transport it himself, without knowing it, of course.

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:10.820
Then, while he's greeting the caretaker,

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:15.210
I'll slide it out and hide it in a hollow of the old apple tree.

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:19.420
Oh, Mr. Boyner, why don't you lay off the stuff for a while?

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:21.780
I may never touch it while I'm there, not a drop.

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:25.120
What you don't understand, all of you, is that I've got to know what's around,

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:27.630
that I can have it if I need it. I can't be cut off completely.

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:29.980
That's the devil. That's what drives you crazy.

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:32.650
Yeah, I know. I know a lot of guys like that.

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:34.730
They take a bottle and put it on a shelf.

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:36.700
All they want to do is just look at it.

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:38.880
They don't even take a corkscrew alone just to make sure.

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:43.710
And all of a sudden, they grab the bottle right off the neck.

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:46.370
Nat, one more reproving word.

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:48.230
I shall consult our lawyer about a divorce.

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:50.200
Now, don't forget quarter of six.

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:52.680
My brother must find me home ready and packed.

00:15:56.260 --> 00:15:56.960
Shall we dance?

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:01.160
He's awfully pretty, Mr. Burnham.

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:03.210
I bet you tell that to all the boys.

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:06.010
Why, Nat? Only with you, it's on the level.

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:07.300
Yeah, sit down.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:10.390
No, thanks. Thanks a lot, but no thanks.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:11.680
There's somebody waiting.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:15.220
Him? I bet he wears arch supporters.

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:17.020
Oh, he's just an old friend of the folks.

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:20.110
Lovely gentleman. He buys me dimpled scotch.

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:23.330
He should buy you Indian rubies in the villa in Calcutta,

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:24.410
overlooking the Ganges.

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:25.450
Don't be ridiculous.

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:29.960
Gloria, please, why imperil our friendship with his loathsome abbreviation?

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.140
I could make myself free for later on if you want.

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:37.410
No, Gloria, I'm going away for the weekend. Some other time.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:41.890
Anytime. I'm just crazy about the back of your hair.

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:56.350
Don't worry about me. Just let me know when it's a quarter of six.

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:59.920
Come on, join me one little jigger of dreams, huh?

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:02.360
No, thank you. You don't approve of drinking?

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:04.680
Not the way you drink.

00:17:05.580 --> 00:17:06.840
It shrinks my liver, doesn't it?

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:09.150
It pickles my kidneys, yes.

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:11.700
But what does it do to my mind?

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:15.880
It tosses the sandbags overboard so the balloon can soar.

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:17.740
Suddenly, I'm above the ordinary.

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:19.420
I'm competent, supremely competent.

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:22.010
I'm walking a tightrope over Niagara Falls.

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:23.660
I am one of the great ones.

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:26.650
I'm Michelangelo, molding the beard of Moses.

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:28.860
I'm Van Gogh, painting pure sunlight.

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:29.640
I'm Horowitz.

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:34.750
and John Barrymore, before the movies got him by the throne.

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:37.660
I'm Jesse James and his two brothers, all three of them.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:39.660
W. Shakespeare.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:43.120
And out there, it's not Third Avenue any longer.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:44.180
It's the Nile, Nat.

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:48.050
The Nile, and down at the barge of Cleopatra.

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:55.160
The purple of the sails, and so perfumers

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:56.520
