00:37:02.690 --> 00:37:04.710
You know, to be really safe, we should go together.

00:37:05.400 --> 00:37:05.820
We could.

00:37:06.740 --> 00:37:07.480
Are you in the phone book?

00:37:08.740 --> 00:37:10.260
Yes, but I'm not home very much.

00:37:10.400 --> 00:37:11.440
Well, I'll call you at your office.

00:37:13.540 --> 00:37:15.600
Editorial research. If Henry Luce answers, hang up.

00:37:15.740 --> 00:37:21.160
All right. Taxi?

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:22.720
No, thanks. I'm taking the subway.

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:23.660
Oh, very sensible.

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:27.360
As a matter of fact, I'm going to an extremely crazy party on Washington Square.

00:37:28.080 --> 00:37:29.400
If you like, I'll take you along.

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:33.380
Well, thank you very much, Miss St. James, but I have to see a friend uptown.

00:37:36.070 --> 00:37:36.910
Goodbye, Mr. Burnham.

00:37:37.110 --> 00:37:37.390
Goodbye.

00:37:51.320 --> 00:37:51.680
Through that.

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:53.220
It fell out of my pocket.

00:37:53.900 --> 00:37:54.840
Do you always carry those things?

00:37:55.200 --> 00:37:55.720
Well, no.

00:37:55.940 --> 00:37:58.640
You see, that friend of mine, the one uptown, he has a slight cold,

00:37:58.680 --> 00:37:59.980
and I thought I'd take this along and make him...

00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:03.220
We'll see that he gets a hot lemonade and some aspirin.

00:38:03.320 --> 00:38:03.760
I shall.

00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:04.300
Goodbye.

00:38:04.940 --> 00:38:09.540
Oh, Miss St. James.

00:38:09.740 --> 00:38:14.340
What kind of a party was that you asked me to?

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:15.500
A cocktail party.

00:38:16.190 --> 00:38:17.110
Invitations still stand?

00:38:17.350 --> 00:38:18.250
Of course, come on.

00:38:26.870 --> 00:38:29.290
Okay, so they go to that cocktail party and he gets stink off.

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:29.970
Floors flatten his...

00:38:30.030 --> 00:38:33.660
He does not. By this time, he's crazy about the girl.

00:38:34.150 --> 00:38:36.910
He drinks tomato juice, doesn't touch liquor that whole week,

00:38:37.090 --> 00:38:38.490
for two weeks, for six weeks.

00:38:38.650 --> 00:38:39.370
And love, huh?

00:38:41.460 --> 00:38:42.960
That's what's gonna be so hard to write.

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:45.890
Love is the hardest thing in the world to write about.

00:38:46.770 --> 00:38:49.560
It's so simple. You've gotta catch it through details.

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:53.240
Like the early morning sunlight hitting the gray tin of the rain spout

00:38:53.240 --> 00:38:54.200
in front of her house.

00:38:54.950 --> 00:38:57.770
The ringing of a telephone that sounds like Beethoven's Pastoral.

00:38:59.110 --> 00:38:59.970
A letter scribbled on a...

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:03.830
that you carry around in your pocket because it smells like all the lilacs in Ohio.

00:39:05.850 --> 00:39:11.200
Pour it, Nat. Thinks he's cured.

00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:15.420
If he could only get a job now, they could be married, and that's that.

00:39:16.090 --> 00:39:17.910
But it's not, Nat. Not quite.

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:21.630
Because one day, one terrible day...

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:23.000
Yeah, go on.

00:39:25.690 --> 00:39:28.090
You see, this girl's been writing to her people in Toledo.

00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:29.960
They want to meet the young man.

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:33.300
So they come to New York. They stay at the Hotel Manhattan.

00:39:34.300 --> 00:39:37.300
Their very first day, she's to introduce him to her parents.

00:39:37.960 --> 00:39:40.230
One o'clock, lobby at the hotel.

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:03.720
They gave me a shampoo, scalp massage, and a manicure.

00:40:04.110 --> 00:40:06.510
I thought they were going to tear off my shoes and paint my toenails.

00:40:07.590 --> 00:40:10.800
I had a lovely morning. Just a little window shopping.

00:40:11.050 --> 00:40:12.430
Didn't want to get all tired out.

00:40:12.590 --> 00:40:15.130
On account of meeting that young man? Mother!

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:16.910
Who did you get that haircut for?

00:40:18.230 --> 00:40:20.290
I wonder what's keeping Helen. He'll be here.

00:40:20.880 --> 00:40:22.970
There's a Burnham fellow. Went to Cornell, didn't he?

00:40:23.070 --> 00:40:23.750
I believe so.

00:40:23.890 --> 00:40:27.420
But he never graduated. I wonder why. How old is he?

00:40:27.660 --> 00:40:28.160
Thirty-three.

00:40:28.550 --> 00:40:29.970
He has no job, as far as I...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:31.300
I can find out he never had one.

00:40:32.020 --> 00:40:33.720
I wish Helen wasn't so vague.

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:35.260
Maybe he has a little money.

00:40:35.660 --> 00:40:36.880
Some people do, you know, Father.

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:38.200
He ought to have a job, anyway.

00:40:38.630 --> 00:40:39.370
He's a writer.

00:40:39.810 --> 00:40:40.850
Writer? What did he write?

00:40:41.030 --> 00:40:42.010
I never heard his name.

00:40:42.290 --> 00:40:43.610
Now, Father, relax.

00:40:44.030 --> 00:40:45.730
You always expect the worst.

00:40:45.910 --> 00:40:48.170
I hope he realizes that Helen's our only dog,

00:40:48.830 --> 00:40:50.010
to know a few things about him.

00:40:50.330 --> 00:40:51.550
How has law come out?

00:40:51.790 --> 00:40:55.250
His background, his prospects, his church affiliations.

00:40:56.130 --> 00:40:57.710
I've made a plan for this.

00:41:36.810 --> 00:41:39.200
Patel Manhattan, your police page, Miss Helenson James.

00:41:39.710 --> 00:41:40.370
St. James.

00:41:40.780 --> 00:41:41.640
Yes, she's in the lobby.

00:41:59.280 --> 00:41:59.440
Done.

00:42:00.160 --> 00:42:02.800
Darling, I'm terribly sorry, but I won't be able to get there for a while.

00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:05.970
Will you please go ahead and have your lunch and apologize to your parents?

00:42:07.520 --> 00:42:10.080
Oh, nothing serious. I'll be there. Goodbye.

00:42:32.940 --> 00:42:34.340
Turn off that light.

00:42:34.860 --> 00:42:35.240
Don.

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:36.720
Turn it off.

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:38.280
For heaven's sake, Don.

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:43.450
I thought you were with Helen and her father and mother.

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:45.520
What happened?

00:42:51.640 --> 00:42:52.600
Couldn't face it.

00:42:52.740 --> 00:42:53.420
Couldn't face what?

00:42:53.520 --> 00:42:54.220
Didn't you go to see them?

00:42:54.660 --> 00:42:55.600
Certainly I went.

00:42:55.740 --> 00:42:56.800
One o'clock sharp.

00:42:57.000 --> 00:42:58.280
I saw them, all right.

00:42:58.640 --> 00:42:59.880
Only they didn't see me.

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:00.590
How was that?

00:43:01.210 --> 00:43:03.370
Such nice, respectable people.

00:43:03.550 --> 00:43:04.570
I couldn't face them.

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:07.250
Wick and all the questions they'd ask me, I just couldn't do it.

00:43:07.310 --> 00:43:07.950
Not cold.

00:43:08.310 --> 00:43:10.210
I had to have a drink for us, just one.

00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:12.480
Only the one didn't do anything to me.

00:43:12.780 --> 00:43:14.060
So you had another and another.

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:15.540
Oh, you poor idiot, Don.

00:43:15.990 --> 00:43:19.770
Won't you ever learn that with you it's like stepping off a roof and expecting to fall just one floor?

00:43:20.090 --> 00:43:21.190
Will you call her, Wick?

00:43:22.370 --> 00:43:23.110
Tell her something.

00:43:23.290 --> 00:43:24.070
Tell her I'm sick.

00:43:24.210 --> 00:43:25.030
Tell her I'm dead.

00:43:25.410 --> 00:43:26.090
Will you call?

00:43:26.690 --> 00:43:27.610
Yes, I'll call.

00:43:27.850 --> 00:43:29.910
You know, she must have written them a lot of nice things.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:33.810
What a gentleman I am, a prince.

00:43:34.120 --> 00:43:34.980
Which hotel is it?

00:43:35.460 --> 00:43:39.620
The Manhattan, Mr. and Mrs. Charles St. James of Toledo, Ohio.

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:59.980
Just a minute.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:00.240
What's that, Helen?

00:44:14.270 --> 00:44:15.110
Wick, is Don here?

00:44:15.370 --> 00:44:16.440
Don? No.

00:44:16.680 --> 00:44:17.660
Any idea where he could be?

00:44:18.770 --> 00:44:19.490
Isn't he meeting you?

00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:22.490
He was supposed to meet us for lunch and then he telephoned he'd be late.

00:44:23.420 --> 00:44:25.180
Mother's beginning to think I just made him up.

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:28.200
Do you suppose something happened to him?

00:44:28.480 --> 00:44:28.840
Nonsense.

00:44:30.220 --> 00:44:31.720
He'd have called back if we were all right.

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:32.640
Where'd he call you from?

00:44:32.800 --> 00:44:33.340
I don't know.

00:44:33.840 --> 00:44:34.720
I think I have an idea.

00:44:34.820 --> 00:44:35.800
He called you from out of town.

00:44:36.720 --> 00:44:37.540
Out of town? Where?

00:44:37.720 --> 00:44:38.000
Philadelphia.

00:44:38.470 --> 00:44:39.490
What's he doing in Philadelphia?

00:44:39.670 --> 00:44:41.590
Well, there's an opening on the Philadelphia Inquirer,

00:44:41.630 --> 00:44:43.730
the book section, and Don wrote them, he wired them,

00:44:43.850 --> 00:44:45.430
and I think this morning he just took an early train.

00:44:46.590 --> 00:44:49.560
Oh, well, he didn't tell me a word about it.

00:44:50.510 --> 00:44:51.870
I'm not supposed to tell you either.

00:44:51.910 --> 00:44:52.830
He wanted it to be a surprise.

00:44:53.540 --> 00:44:53.900
He did?

00:44:54.140 --> 00:44:56.820
Yes, he probably couldn't meet the right people right away,

00:44:57.020 --> 00:44:58.280
Mr. Train, you know how it is.

00:44:58.710 --> 00:44:59.970
Oh, it would be wonderful if he...

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:01.260
He got the job and started working.

00:45:04.080 --> 00:45:07.350
Or would it, Wick? With him in Philadelphia and me in New York.

00:45:08.800 --> 00:45:10.360
Don't ever tell him I said that, though, will you?

00:45:10.500 --> 00:45:11.200
Of course not.

00:45:12.500 --> 00:45:14.320
I can never understand why somebody like Don,

00:45:14.420 --> 00:45:17.280
a person with such talent, such flashes of real brilliance.

00:45:18.470 --> 00:45:19.690
Maybe I'm a bit prejudiced.

00:45:19.850 --> 00:45:22.730
What are you doing, Wick?

00:45:23.690 --> 00:45:23.970
Nothing.

00:45:24.300 --> 00:45:25.460
Where did that bottle come from?

00:45:25.880 --> 00:45:26.940
It just rolled out.

00:45:27.060 --> 00:45:27.780
From under the couch?

00:45:28.670 --> 00:45:29.270
Yes, Helen.

00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:33.400
You know, it's my guess that Don caught an early train and...

00:45:33.400 --> 00:45:34.300
Is that Don's bottle?

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:35.940
What makes you think that?

00:45:36.120 --> 00:45:37.740
There was a bottle the first time we met.

00:45:37.920 --> 00:45:38.060
What?

00:45:40.280 --> 00:45:41.400
It fell out of Don's pocket.

00:45:41.620 --> 00:45:43.680
That was for me, Helen.

00:45:44.200 --> 00:45:45.140
This one's mine, too.

00:45:46.640 --> 00:45:48.540
You might as well hear the family scandal.

00:45:49.220 --> 00:45:49.760
I drink.

00:45:50.230 --> 00:45:51.390
Don thinks I drink too much.

00:45:51.510 --> 00:45:52.690
I had to promise to go on the wagon.

00:45:52.810 --> 00:45:54.470
That's why I hid the bottle so he wouldn't see it.

00:45:54.910 --> 00:45:56.150
Oh, I'm sorry, Wick.

00:45:56.990 --> 00:45:58.950
I shouldn't have started asking questions.

00:45:59.510 --> 00:45:59.970
It's really not...

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:07.020
of my business forget it helen i'd better be getting back to the hotel don's probably there

00:46:07.020 --> 00:46:12.290
already and don't worry wick i won't mention a word of it to him thank you bye bye helen

00:46:12.290 --> 00:46:22.300
i'm sorry helen i can't let you go not like this don shut your mouth don i'll take you downstairs

00:46:22.300 --> 00:46:29.710
thanks very much for your philadelphia story wick nice try that looks so silly on you don't listen to

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:32.140
She doesn't have to. Just look at the two of us.

00:46:32.640 --> 00:46:34.480
Yes, what is all this covering up?

00:46:34.680 --> 00:46:37.320
All that happened is that Don was nervous at the idea of meeting your parents,

00:46:37.420 --> 00:46:38.360
and so he had a couple of drinks.

00:46:38.660 --> 00:46:41.100
Come on, Wick. She'd have found out sooner or later.

00:46:41.720 --> 00:46:42.820
Stop it, both of you.

00:46:44.640 --> 00:46:46.580
Don's a little tight. Most people drink a little.

00:46:46.700 --> 00:46:48.200
A lot of them get tight once in a while.

00:46:48.760 --> 00:46:50.940
Sure, the lucky ones who can take it or leave it.

00:46:51.330 --> 00:46:54.330
But then there are the ones who can't take it and can't leave it either.

00:46:54.960 --> 00:46:58.220
What I'm trying to say is I'm not a drinker, I'm a drunk.

00:46:59.040 --> 00:46:59.920
They had to put me away.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:01.160
He went to a cure.

00:47:01.360 --> 00:47:02.300
Which didn't take.

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:07.780
You see, that first time we met, I should have had the decency to get drunk just for your sake.

00:47:07.980 --> 00:47:08.680
For my sake?

00:47:08.940 --> 00:47:10.020
We're talking about you.

00:47:11.100 --> 00:47:12.340
Is it really that bad, Wick?

00:47:12.680 --> 00:47:13.580
Yes, it is.

00:47:14.200 --> 00:47:16.600
Can't we go over this tomorrow, Don, when you're feeling more like yourself?

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:18.540
Helen's heard the facts.

00:47:18.680 --> 00:47:19.860
That's all there is to it.

00:47:20.230 --> 00:47:21.970
Yes, I've heard them, and they're not too pleasant.

00:47:23.000 --> 00:47:23.940
But they could be worse.

00:47:24.120 --> 00:47:25.960
After all, you're not an embezzler or a murderer.

00:47:26.120 --> 00:47:27.720
You drink too much, and that's not fatal.

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:29.720
One cure didn't take, there are others.

00:47:30.220 --> 00:47:33.070
Of course there are. This has a familiar ring.

00:47:33.330 --> 00:47:36.730
But there must be a reason why you drink, Don. The right doctor could find it.

00:47:36.850 --> 00:47:39.670
Look, I'm way ahead of the right doctor. I know the reason.

00:47:39.770 --> 00:47:43.410
The reason is me, what I am. Or rather, what I'm not.

00:47:43.820 --> 00:47:45.600
What I wanted to become and didn't.

00:47:45.770 --> 00:47:47.950
What is it you want to be so much that you're not?

00:47:50.220 --> 00:47:53.250
A writer. Silly, isn't it?

00:47:56.470 --> 00:47:58.470
You know, in college, I passed for a genius.

00:47:59.030 --> 00:47:59.970
They couldn't get out the...

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:06.990
college magazine without one of my stories. Boy, was I hot. Hemingway stuff. I reached my peak when

00:48:06.990 --> 00:48:13.250
I was 19. Sold the piece, the Atlantic Monthly, reprinted in the Reader's Digest. Who wants to

00:48:13.250 --> 00:48:19.270
stay in college when he's Hemingway? My mother bought me a brand new typewriter, and I moved

00:48:19.270 --> 00:48:25.320
right in on New York. Well, the first thing I wrote, that didn't quite come off, and the second

00:48:25.320 --> 00:48:29.980
I dropped, the public wasn't ready for that one. I started a third, and...

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:40.300
By then somebody began to look over my shoulder and whisper in a thin, clear voice like the E-string on a violin.

00:48:41.740 --> 00:48:47.480
Don Burner made whisper? It's not good enough. Not that way.

00:48:49.880 --> 00:48:52.680
How about a couple of drinks just to set it on his feet, huh?

00:48:54.090 --> 00:48:59.970
So I had a couple. Oh, what a great idea that was. That made all the difference. Suddenly I could say...

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:04.420
See, the whole thing, the tragic sweep of the great novel, beautifully proportioned.

00:49:05.400 --> 00:49:08.120
But before I could really grab it and throw it down on paper,

00:49:08.240 --> 00:49:11.260
the drinks would wear off and everything would be gone like a mirage.

00:49:12.450 --> 00:49:15.610
And there was despair and a drink to counterbalance despair

00:49:15.610 --> 00:49:18.170
and then one to counterbalance the counterbalance.

00:49:19.680 --> 00:49:25.160
And I'd sit in front of that typewriter trying to squeeze out one page that was halfway decent

00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:27.720
and that guy would pop up again.

00:49:29.150 --> 00:49:29.970
What guy? Who are you?

00:49:30.680 --> 00:49:35.960
The other Don Vernon. There are two of us, you know, Don the drunk and Don the writer.

00:49:37.200 --> 00:49:38.900
And the drunk would say to the writer,

00:49:39.970 --> 00:49:43.290
Let's get some good out of that portable. Let's hock it.

00:49:43.670 --> 00:49:46.190
Let's take it to that pawn shop over on Third Avenue.

00:49:46.190 --> 00:49:47.750
It's always good for $10.

00:49:48.550 --> 00:49:52.430
Another drink, another binge, another bender, another spree.

00:49:53.370 --> 00:49:54.950
Such humorous words.

00:49:56.380 --> 00:49:59.440
I try to break away from that guy a lot of times, but...

00:50:01.220 --> 00:50:05.540
You know, once I even got myself a gun and some bullets.

00:50:05.920 --> 00:50:08.240
I was gonna do it on my 30th birthday.

00:50:09.960 --> 00:50:10.780
Yeah, the bullets.

00:50:11.920 --> 00:50:13.980
My gun went for three quarts of whiskey.

00:50:14.440 --> 00:50:16.340
That other Don wanted us to have a drink.

00:50:17.050 --> 00:50:19.450
He always wants us to have a drink first.

00:50:20.860 --> 00:50:23.120
Flop suicide of a flop rider.

00:50:23.420 --> 00:50:24.580
All right, maybe you're not a rider.

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:25.620
Why don't you do something else?

00:50:26.140 --> 00:50:27.860
Sure, take a nice job.

00:50:28.080 --> 00:50:29.960
Public accountant, real estate...

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:35.210
I haven't the guts, Helen. Most men lead lives of quiet desperation.

00:50:35.550 --> 00:50:37.030
I can't take quiet desperation.

00:50:37.190 --> 00:50:40.890
But you are a writer. You have every quality for it, imagination, wit, pity.

00:50:41.780 --> 00:50:46.880
Come on, let's face reality. I'm 33. I'm living on the charity of my brother.

00:50:47.390 --> 00:50:50.790
Roman board free, 50 cents a week for cigarettes,

00:50:50.970 --> 00:50:53.190
an occasional ticket to a show or a concert,

00:50:53.640 --> 00:50:58.220
all out of the bigness of his heart. And it is a big heart and a patient one.

00:50:58.440 --> 00:50:59.980
Now, Don, I've only been carrying your lo-

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:03.460
Shut up, Wick! I've never done anything. I'm not doing anything.

00:51:03.600 --> 00:51:06.500
I never will do anything. Zero, zero, zero.

00:51:06.620 --> 00:51:08.280
No, you shut up. We'll straighten it out.

00:51:08.640 --> 00:51:12.370
Look, Wick has the misfortune to be my brother.

00:51:12.550 --> 00:51:14.270
You just happened to walk in on this.

00:51:14.430 --> 00:51:17.490
Now, if you know what's good for you, you'll turn around and walk out again.

00:51:17.650 --> 00:51:19.690
And walk fast. And don't turn back.

00:51:25.680 --> 00:51:27.600
Make some coffee, Wick. Strong. Three cups.

00:51:28.590 --> 00:51:29.960
Look, Helen, do you...

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:32.060
Do yourself a favor. Go on, clear out.

00:51:32.860 --> 00:51:35.340
Because I've got a rival? Because you're in love with this?

00:51:35.980 --> 00:51:41.020
You don't know me, Don. I'm gonna fight and fight and fight. Bend down.

00:51:58.250 --> 00:51:59.130
Years ago, Nat.

00:52:02.000 --> 00:52:04.910
She knows she's clutching a razor blade, but she won't let go.

00:52:05.800 --> 00:52:06.660
Three years of it.

00:52:07.800 --> 00:52:09.580
And what? How does it come out?

00:52:10.000 --> 00:52:11.620
I don't know. I haven't figured that far.

00:52:12.300 --> 00:52:12.900
Want me to tell you?

