01:29:52.200 --> 01:29:53.680
You're not making much sense, Don.

01:29:54.640 --> 01:29:56.320
And you know what Nat said about the ending?

01:29:58.370 --> 01:29:59.090
Like this.

01:30:01.020 --> 01:30:05.920
Like that. This. Like that.

01:31:06.330 --> 01:31:07.490
It's all right, Don. Give me the pawn ticket.

01:31:07.730 --> 01:31:08.430
No scene, please.

01:31:08.590 --> 01:31:10.010
No scene. Just give me the pawn ticket.

01:31:10.270 --> 01:31:12.390
Look, I don't want you to go in there and claim it now. It'd look queer.

01:31:12.590 --> 01:31:14.190
You're ashamed of what the pawnbroker will think.

01:31:14.330 --> 01:31:15.810
It doesn't matter what I think.

01:31:15.910 --> 01:31:17.030
When can you get your back your coat?

01:31:17.150 --> 01:31:20.610
You couldn't have taken my bracelet or my paycheck. It had to be that coat.

01:31:20.950 --> 01:31:23.770
You mean the one that brought us together? Stop being sentimental.

01:31:24.030 --> 01:31:26.590
Oh, I have, Don. I assure you, it's finished. It's dead.

01:31:26.930 --> 01:31:29.970
For three years, they couldn't talk me out of you. I was the only one that...

01:31:30.600 --> 01:31:32.580
I knew there was a core of something.

01:31:32.780 --> 01:31:34.960
Well, there is a core, and now I know what it is.

01:31:35.140 --> 01:31:35.820
A sponge.

01:31:36.220 --> 01:31:37.880
And to soak it full, you'll do anything

01:31:37.880 --> 01:31:39.660
that's ruthless, selfish, dishonest.

01:31:39.740 --> 01:31:40.760
I asked you now to make a scene.

01:31:40.900 --> 01:31:41.640
Give me the ticket.

01:31:41.820 --> 01:31:42.820
No, Helen, not now, please.

01:31:43.080 --> 01:31:43.920
I don't want the money.

01:31:44.000 --> 01:31:45.920
You can get as drunk as you like for all I care.

01:31:47.080 --> 01:31:47.580
Thank you.

01:32:00.090 --> 01:32:02.490
A gentleman came in a while ago. How much did you give him for that coat?

01:32:02.770 --> 01:32:03.090
Why?

01:32:03.370 --> 01:32:04.750
Well, I want it back. It's my coat.

01:32:04.930 --> 01:32:05.630
It's your coat?

01:32:05.730 --> 01:32:07.690
Oh, it's all right. He had my permission. How much did you give him?

01:32:07.930 --> 01:32:09.930
He didn't want any money. He just wanted to swap it.

01:32:10.030 --> 01:32:10.830
Swap it for what?

01:32:11.400 --> 01:32:13.380
Oh, something he hocked her a long while back.

01:32:13.670 --> 01:32:13.930
What?

01:32:14.550 --> 01:32:24.060
A gun. Now, if you want the coat back, I can...

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:30.360
What's the matter?

01:33:34.440 --> 01:33:34.680
Nothing.

01:33:36.960 --> 01:33:37.760
Dave gave me the keys.

01:33:37.880 --> 01:33:38.720
I didn't think you were here.

01:33:42.390 --> 01:33:43.110
What do you want here?

01:33:44.220 --> 01:33:46.600
It's just that the rain is worse and I couldn't get a taxi.

01:33:46.860 --> 01:33:49.990
I thought perhaps I could borrow a coat under the circumstances.

01:33:50.530 --> 01:33:50.830
Sure.

01:33:51.390 --> 01:33:52.310
How about my raincoat?

01:33:58.940 --> 01:33:59.980
Funny that we should wind up...

01:34:00.000 --> 01:34:06.480
after all these years just as we met i with your raincoat and i with your leopard coat i always

01:34:06.480 --> 01:34:16.420
got the best of the bargain goodbye what are you looking for i thought perhaps maybe you might

01:34:16.420 --> 01:34:21.350
have something from my hair would you care to wear my black derby any old thing any old scarf

01:34:41.290 --> 01:34:42.590
Thank you. So long.

01:34:45.060 --> 01:34:48.080
Do you know, Don, there was some whiskey left in the bottle after I cleaned up last night.

01:34:48.240 --> 01:34:48.700
Was there?

01:34:49.250 --> 01:34:50.530
Wouldn't you like to know where I put it?

01:34:50.790 --> 01:34:50.950
No.

01:34:52.220 --> 01:34:53.040
Do you want a drink, Don?

01:34:53.380 --> 01:34:53.640
No.

01:34:57.760 --> 01:34:59.970
Here it is, right here. Why don't you have one?

01:35:00.000 --> 01:35:02.960
Just one. What are you up to?

01:35:04.480 --> 01:35:07.300
Nothing. I'm just ashamed of the way I talk to you.

01:35:07.530 --> 01:35:10.390
Like a narrow-minded, insensitive, small-town teetotaler.

01:35:11.010 --> 01:35:12.570
I told you I don't feel like a drink. Not now.

01:35:13.130 --> 01:35:15.510
Come on, Don. Just one. I'll have one with you.

01:35:15.610 --> 01:35:17.510
I'm in no hurry. This is my easy day at the office.

01:35:17.730 --> 01:35:20.390
Look, Helen, there are a few things I want to put in order before week comes.

01:35:21.200 --> 01:35:23.090
Let me stay. Please.

01:35:23.510 --> 01:35:29.420
No. I don't want to sound rude, but I'm afraid you'll have to leave now.

01:35:31.080 --> 01:35:31.580
Here, Don.

01:35:33.610 --> 01:35:34.370
You're very sweet.

01:35:37.610 --> 01:35:37.970
Goodbye.

01:35:41.700 --> 01:35:42.660
There'll be Ben for nothing.

01:35:43.070 --> 01:35:43.870
You need this, Don.

01:35:44.010 --> 01:35:44.270
Drink it.

01:35:44.310 --> 01:35:45.210
I want you to drink it.

01:35:45.540 --> 01:35:46.260
I'll get you some more.

01:35:46.300 --> 01:35:47.220
I'll get you all you want.

01:35:47.640 --> 01:35:48.640
What kind of talk is that?

01:35:50.120 --> 01:35:52.180
Just that I'd rather have you drunk than dead.

01:35:52.380 --> 01:35:55.820
Who wants to be dead?

01:35:56.840 --> 01:35:57.900
Stop lying to me.

01:36:00.000 --> 01:36:12.740
Now, go. Don't fuss, please. Don't call in the neighbors. It won't do any good. I promise you.

01:36:13.140 --> 01:36:17.460
I won't. You've made up for mine. Could you tell me why? Why?

01:36:17.780 --> 01:36:20.260
Because it's best all around for everybody, for you, for Wick and for me.

01:36:20.300 --> 01:36:21.940
But that's not true. We love you, Wick and I.

01:36:21.940 --> 01:36:24.250
All right, then, for me. Selfish again.

01:36:24.970 --> 01:36:26.690
It's a sad final word, Don.

01:36:26.930 --> 01:36:29.970
Look at it this way, Helen. This business is just a formality. Don Burnham is dead.

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:31.620
He died over this weekend.

01:36:31.910 --> 01:36:33.390
Did he? What did he die of?

01:36:33.660 --> 01:36:34.360
A lot of things.

01:36:34.820 --> 01:36:38.200
Of alcohol, of moral anemia, fear, shame, DTs.

01:36:39.780 --> 01:36:40.940
That Don Burnham.

01:36:41.480 --> 01:36:42.720
And now you want to kill the other one?

01:36:42.940 --> 01:36:43.400
What other?

01:36:43.640 --> 01:36:45.360
There were two Dons. You told me so yourself.

01:36:45.500 --> 01:36:46.720
Don the drunk and Don the writer.

01:36:47.390 --> 01:36:49.350
Let's not go back to a fancy figure of speech.

01:36:49.510 --> 01:36:50.270
There's only one Don.

01:36:52.000 --> 01:36:52.280
True.

01:36:54.100 --> 01:36:56.200
I'm all right. I still have enough strength left.

01:36:56.650 --> 01:36:57.930
I know you have. I can see it.

01:36:58.310 --> 01:36:59.870
Don't waste it by pulling a trigger.

01:37:00.160 --> 01:37:01.540
Oh, let me get it over with.

01:37:01.620 --> 01:37:03.780
Oh, you want me to give you another one of my promises that I never keep?

01:37:03.980 --> 01:37:05.340
I don't want you to give me your promise.

01:37:05.480 --> 01:37:08.000
I don't want you to give your promise to anybody but Don Burnham.

01:37:08.200 --> 01:37:09.960
It's too late. I wouldn't know how to start.

01:37:10.220 --> 01:37:11.780
The only way to start is to stop.

01:37:12.180 --> 01:37:14.140
There is no cure besides just stopping.

01:37:14.510 --> 01:37:15.170
Can't be done.

01:37:15.480 --> 01:37:16.580
Other people have stopped.

01:37:16.860 --> 01:37:18.520
People with a purpose, with something to do.

01:37:18.890 --> 01:37:20.330
But you've got talent and ambition.

01:37:21.100 --> 01:37:23.360
Talent, ambition, that's dead long ago. That's drowned.

01:37:24.140 --> 01:37:26.800
That's drifting around with a bloated belly in a lake of alcohol.

01:37:27.420 --> 01:37:28.980
No, it isn't. You still have it.

01:37:29.200 --> 01:37:29.960
Quit trying to stop.

01:37:30.000 --> 01:37:32.680
Helen, it's too late. There's no more writing left in me. It's gone.

01:37:32.820 --> 01:37:33.700
What do you expect? A miracle?

01:37:33.980 --> 01:37:36.400
Yes, yes, yes. If I could just make you un...

01:37:36.400 --> 01:37:37.510
Who is it?

01:37:38.020 --> 01:37:39.200
It's me, Mr. Blaynham.

01:37:39.420 --> 01:37:39.920
What is it, Ned?

01:37:40.940 --> 01:37:42.120
I've got something for you, Mr.

01:37:46.930 --> 01:37:47.970
Intruder. What is it?

01:37:48.780 --> 01:37:52.500
You know, when you had that accident, when I found this floating around on the Nile.

01:37:53.470 --> 01:37:54.790
She writes pretty good. I oiled her up a little.

01:37:55.450 --> 01:37:57.030
I didn't oiled her up so you can hock her.

01:38:00.000 --> 01:38:00.700
I'll take it, Nat.

01:38:01.010 --> 01:38:04.330
Hello, Miss. Thank you, Nat.

01:38:05.170 --> 01:38:06.530
How are all them lilacs in Ohio?

01:38:10.780 --> 01:38:12.400
Well, Don, here it is. What do you say now?

01:38:13.240 --> 01:38:14.060
Say about what?

01:38:14.390 --> 01:38:16.460
This. Someone somewhere sent it back.

01:38:16.720 --> 01:38:18.100
Why? Because he means you to stay alive.

01:38:18.260 --> 01:38:19.160
Because he wants you to write.

01:38:19.320 --> 01:38:20.800
I didn't ask for a big miracle.

01:38:21.050 --> 01:38:23.630
Write with these hands and a brain that's all out of focus?

01:38:23.770 --> 01:38:25.230
It'll clear up again. You'll be well.

01:38:25.390 --> 01:38:27.950
I'll be sitting there staring at that white sheet, scared.

01:38:28.130 --> 01:38:29.970
No, you won't. You've forgotten what a...

01:38:30.720 --> 01:38:32.080
What am I going to write about?

01:38:32.300 --> 01:38:32.520
What?

01:38:32.720 --> 01:38:34.140
What you've always wanted to write.

01:38:34.640 --> 01:38:35.760
Where was the page I found?

01:38:37.030 --> 01:38:39.210
The Bottle, a novel by Don Burnham.

01:38:39.500 --> 01:38:40.340
What was that to be?

01:38:40.640 --> 01:38:43.700
About a messed up life, about a man and a woman and a bottle,

01:38:43.940 --> 01:38:46.080
about nightmares, horrors, humiliations,

01:38:46.260 --> 01:38:47.580
all the things I want to forget.

01:38:47.840 --> 01:38:50.000
Put it all down on paper. Get rid of it that way.

01:38:50.100 --> 01:38:51.840
Tell it all to whom it may concern.

01:38:52.080 --> 01:38:54.000
And it concerns so many people, Don.

01:38:54.340 --> 01:38:54.600
Here.

01:38:56.520 --> 01:38:57.640
I'll fix us some breakfast.

01:39:00.320 --> 01:39:01.920
We have quite a supply of milk.

01:39:04.960 --> 01:39:07.200
You'll notice I didn't even find the first line.

01:39:07.730 --> 01:39:10.580
Of course, you couldn't write the beginning because you didn't know the ending.

01:39:11.160 --> 01:39:29.960
Only now, only now you know.

01:39:30.000 --> 01:39:39.570
I'm gonna send one copy to Bim, one to the doctor who loaned me his coat, and one to Nat.

01:39:41.320 --> 01:39:43.480
Imagine Wick standing in front of a bookstore.

01:39:44.210 --> 01:39:46.010
A great big pyramid of my books.

01:39:46.600 --> 01:39:48.200
A novel by Don Burnham.

01:39:49.180 --> 01:39:50.420
That's by my brother, you know.

01:39:50.730 --> 01:39:53.010
That's by my fellow. Didn't I always tell you?

01:39:53.500 --> 01:39:56.600
I'm gonna put this whole weekend down, minute by minute.

01:39:56.760 --> 01:39:57.280
Why not?

01:39:57.880 --> 01:39:59.880
The way I stood in there, packing my suitcase.

01:40:01.310 --> 01:40:02.970
Only my mind wasn't on the suitcase.

01:40:03.940 --> 01:40:04.900
It wasn't on the weekend.

01:40:06.110 --> 01:40:08.790
Nor was it on the shirts I was putting in the suitcase, either.

01:40:10.340 --> 01:40:12.340
My mind was hanging outside the window.

01:40:12.580 --> 01:40:15.990
It was suspended just about 18 inches below.

01:40:18.080 --> 01:40:20.700
Out there in that great big concrete jungle,

01:40:20.860 --> 01:40:22.840
I wonder how many others there are like me.

01:40:24.580 --> 01:40:26.840
Deviled guys on fire with thirst.

01:40:28.140 --> 01:40:29.980
Such comical figures to the rest of the world.

01:40:30.380 --> 01:40:35.040
They stagger blindly towards another binge, another bender, another spring.
